ghts the  incongruity between the person she feels  she is on the inside  and the image she has to project on the  outside for much of the film  when mulan looks at herself her  reflection wars with her own perception  of who she is  the choice to frequently portray the  film's two most powerful female  characters through indirect means helps  convey their internal struggles with  self-image  and their fears of showing the world who  they truly are  kids probably won't think to question  mulan's unswerving devotion to the  emperor and his dynasty  but adult viewers may wonder if mulan's  cause is quite as just as she believes  after mulan is banished from her  regiment shenyang tries to explain to  her that the society  in which they live has no room for  powerful women like them  together she hopes she and mulan may be  able to create a new society that won't  reject them  however mulan doesn't even seem to  consider what chang young is saying  and she rushes off to save the emperor  from bori khan's attack  as a matter of fact mulan never once  seems to interrogate the idea of what  unquestioning fealty to the current  system means for people like her  even though she previously felt stifled  by that very system  the emperor seems like a nice enough guy  but it wouldn't be unreasonable to  wonder whether we should really be  rooting for mulan to safeguard his  dynasty  granted the 1998 film also never  questions the governmental structure  mulan vows to uphold  but in 2020 it seems like a little  healthy introspection may have been both  warranted  and welcome as it stands it seems a  little alarming that mulan fights so  hard to maintain a status quo that  grants tremendous power to men of a  certain bloodline  while keeping women as a subservient  class  while mulan makes friends with all her  fellow soldiers adults won't be able to  miss that the looks hung wei  is giving hua jun contained far more  than brotherly affection  fans who interpreted lee shang as  bisexual in the 1998 film will likely  pick up on the same energy in hong wei  although it's possible hong wei  suspected that mulan was female all  along  the simpler explanation is that hong wei  was simply attracted to huajin  his demeanor toward mulan remains much  the same after her true identity is  revealed  implying that his attraction was never  tied to her gender  it remained constant whether she was  mulan contains a number of impressive  battle sequences  but none of the foes mulan faces loom  nearly as large as the sexism she  encounters in every facet of life  whether she's trying to bring honor to  her family through an arranged marriage  or bring honor to her country through  her skilled fighting as a soldier  she's constantly butting up against the  societal constraints placed upon women  she's told that her powerful qi is a  liability for a woman  although it would be an asset for a man  and during training women are forbidden  from entering the soldiers camp bringing  women into camp  or consorting with women in any way  penalty  death what's more this same sexist  depression is what drove sean young to  side with bori khan in the first place  she tells mulan that she tried to find a  noble path forward  but it didn't exist within an acceptable  society the only options available to  her were to either ally herself with an  enemy of the kingdom  or deny her own power the more power i  showed the more i was quacked just like  you  considering that xiang young is  instrumental in gaining bori khan most  of his tactical advantages during the  film  it's hard to ignore that if society  hadn't been so intent on oppressing  women  the emperor may not have needed saving  in the first place  throughout the film mulan is constantly  trying to embody the three pillars of  virtue  loyal race  and true  early on she strives to live out these  virtues and make her family proud  through restraining her impulses in  order to present herself well  to make a good and respectable match  later she experiences  a crisis of conscience when she realizes  she can't possibly be true  while also deceiving everyone around her  ultimately she comes into her own when  she rejects both the societal  expectations of women  and the pretense of being a man and  embraces her own unique skills and  identity  it's interesting that for the majority  of the film it seems as though everyone  in mulan's life  including mulan herself assumes that  being true  means to accept her place and to stop  bucking traditions and questioning  cultural norms  you are the daughter  learn your place if mulan had remained a  demure and subservient daughter everyone  would have thought that she was being  true to the role that had been given to  her but by the end of the film  we're shown that what's actually true  for mulan is a past that didn't exist  before she carved it out for herself  while kids will likely embrace the  film's themes of self-acceptance on a  surface level  adults are more equipped to unpack the  complex idea that our understanding of  who we are  and how to live truthfully can evolve  over time  in all the promotional material for  milan it seemed as though  shenyang was going to be the main  antagonist of the film  but by the end of the film chanyung has  seen an alternate version of herself  in mulan she ultimately sacrifices  herself to save the younger woman  kids won't miss shenyang's sacrifice but  what they might  fail to see is that shangyoung was never  really a villain at all  shenyang tells mulan that she joined  forces with bori khan  because he was the only one who provided  a place for her and allowed her to be  who she was  but as we see before their encounter  she's really more of a prisoner than a  lieutenant  now i know i serve you  i am the slave and you would do well to  remember it  while she does bori khan's bidding for  much of the film her true motivation is  to create a society in which she can be  accepted without 